{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794586383,"nonce":"wmH30Rc9xup7E1IiSx8//9LqPb0=","message":"Cause: the example, not the engine. scroll_bench put the list in div().w_full().flex_grow() with no minimum height. A flex item's minimum height is its content, so that wrapper grew to the height of every row (150,016 px at 5,000 rows), the scroll's own bounds were that tall, and its viewport was the whole list: nothing to scroll, nothing to cull. Traced in the window: the scroll node recorded an overscan of 150,016 and painted every row (primitive range 0..5000 at 5,000 rows). That is the cached batch holding every row, and the harness never saw it because it sizes its scroll with an explicit height.\n\nFixed in ef27c2a4 (min_h(0.0) on the wrapper). Same window, release, 480x800 at 2x, flat list: 5,000 rows median 2.6 ms (was 14.4), 20,000 rows median 1.3 ms, p99 7.3 ms (was 57 ms median); every scrolled frame on the fast path; the capacity warning is gone. The node now paints 50 primitives at an overscan of 749. Virtual list of 100,000 rows unchanged: every frame a full paint, median 3.2 ms. BLINC_MOTION_PROMOTION=1 at 20,000 rows now runs (full paint each frame, median 3.7 ms).\n\nSo the scroll-as-rows numbers in 3b364ef stand for a list that has a viewport. Not an engine fault. Left over: the baked abort when a frame has more primitives than the buffer, filed as d8f841a. The harness still does not wait for the GPU, so a GPU-side cost would not show in it; no fault found that depends on that.","files":null}]}